{"author":{"id":"038d65070aa747b6bc9d50939fa697ec871a40ed259db54429b255bbef2581d6"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792795219,"nonce":"J37QYm5m7aVSpCSkTGZg4bmQeUI=","title":"Jam: collaborative development, streaming, media, accounts and tiers","message":"Planning baseline: 2026-10-23. This consolidates the requested Jam, platform-independent broadcasting, collaborator audio/video, Cloudflare infrastructure, account management and tiering discussions. It is a roadmap, not implemented functionality or published prices. The associated issues define delivery and acceptance; completing this planning task does not complete those issues.\n\n\nJam is a shared development session inside RayDE. Small teams can collaborate privately, optionally broadcast their work through independently selected platforms, and build/playtest game projects. The collaboration protocol is language- and engine-independent; Haxe project starters, Rayzor and RayDE build tasks provide the first ecosystem integration.\n\nModel an optional Jam event (name, theme, deadline and teams), a team/workspace room (participants, shared files and checkpoints), and per-user broadcasts (provider identity, destination, status and audience chat). Multiple team rooms can belong to one event. Each participant chooses whether and where to broadcast; participants do not need the broadcaster's platform account to collaborate.\n\nStart with small invite-only rooms. A Jam room has one explicit personal/team billing owner. The current host and the payer are separate identities; disconnecting a host must not silently switch who pays. Public viewers cannot edit, execute programs or enter a private call merely because they can watch or send chat messages.\n\n\n1. Start Jam from the command center or Jam entry point. Choose the workspace, explicitly shared file/folder set, name, visibility and optional theme/deadline. Nothing is uploaded merely by signing in.\n2. Invite collaborators using scoped, expiring and revocable invitations. Start with host/moderator, editor and spectator room roles. Count everyone in a room, including its host, toward the applicable limit.\n3. Collaborate with live text edits, remote cursors/selections, participant presence and opt-in follow mode. Preserve each user's own undo semantics. Switching or hiding tabs releases editor presentations while document data remains independent.\n4. Build and playtest from an identified source checkpoint. Share selected build results, diagnostics, logs and artifact links. Explicit host authorization is required for remote build or terminal control. Joining a room never runs received code automatically.\n5. Join a private team call with microphone selection, mute, push-to-talk and speaking indicators. Add optional cameras and explicit game-window/screen sharing. A later browser guest client supports artists, musicians and playtesters without requiring the native IDE.\n6. Optionally connect a streaming provider and an existing OBS profile, preview a scene, then start broadcasting. Keep team chat and provider audience chat identifiable and distinct. Appearing in a private call is separate from permission to appear in a public broadcast.\n7. Finish the session with a durable checkpoint, selected exports/artifact/recording links and orderly cleanup. Local files remain usable when signed out, offline or after a subscription ends.\n\n\n- RayDE native/Haxe application: document model, editor bindings, session UI, local files/build tasks, scoped credential broker and a non-blocking application-level account manager. Workspaces/windows keep independent personal/team context.\n- Ashui CodeEditor: precise edit transaction/origin hooks, index conversion, undo integration and efficient remote presence decorations. Share data rather than editor or rendered-node instances.\n- Native Jam process: Yrs protocol/CRDT state, asynchronous connection work and bounded IPC. Pin interoperability versions and exchange versioned data, never process-local native pointers.\n- Native media process: device capture, audio processing, WebRTC, encoding/decoding and bounded frame ownership. RayScript carries control/state messages; media frames use a designed shared-memory/native-surface transport rather than JSON/base64. Capture and native UI integration require prototypes on each target OS.\n- RayScript extensions: Jam UI contributions, streaming provider adapters and OBS/broadcast-driver controls, using scoped asynchronous service APIs and a lifecycle-aware provider registry. The currently documented generic net.fetch/process.spawn capabilities are placeholders; the foundation must implement the required services.\n- Cloudflare Worker API: authentication handoff, version negotiation, authorization, request routing, scoped asset access and provider callbacks.\n- D1: account/identity directory, personal/team spaces, memberships, device/session metadata, subscription projections and searchable event/room metadata.\n- One SQLite-backed JamRoom Durable Object per team room initially: current membership/permissions, live connections, temporary presence, document update/checkpoint state and media publication ownership.\n- One UsageAccount Durable Object per personal/team billing owner: serialized quota reservations and durable idempotent accounting; D1 contains query/reporting projections.\n- R2: authorized large binary assets, snapshots/exports and build artifacts. References and hashes live in room metadata; do not load an entire game's assets into an object or editor heap.\n- Queues: verified billing events, reconciliation and deferred cleanup. Durable Object alarms schedule actual pending work, not short-interval idle polling.\n- Cloudflare Realtime SFU/TURN: private collaborator media. The room controls who can publish/subscribe and which tracks are offered; media packets go directly between native/browser endpoints and the media service.\n- OBS: local scenes, mixing, capture and public stream encoding. Twitch/YouTube/custom destination APIs provide optional channel, chat, metadata and audience status features. They do not define Jam editing membership.\n\n\nUse a tested CRDT implementation, with Yrs on native clients and a compatible Yjs/Yrs backend candidate. Durable Objects coordinate authority/storage; they do not themselves merge simultaneous edits. Prototype the exact binary/version protocol and document position representation before integration. Preserve correct Unicode/UTF-8/UTF-16 boundaries, multi-cursor edits, formatting, IME composition, code actions and local undo origin.\n\nClients apply local edits responsively and keep bounded unacknowledged updates. The room checks current authorization, persists accepted updates, and acknowledges/broadcasts them. Stable update/request identifiers make retry idempotent. On reconnect exchange state vectors/checkpoints and missing updates; make rejected or irreconcilable edits recoverable rather than silently overwriting a file.\n\nSpecify save ownership, dirty state, disk-change conflicts, file create/rename/delete operations, host handover and what happens if all clients leave. Integrate existing data-only document history rather than allocating editors for remote or inactive files. Server/client crashes must reconstruct accepted state from persisted data. Presence/cursors are temporary and can be discarded on reconnect.\n\nBound update logs and CRDT retention. Define snapshot/epoch negotiation and the treatment of very old offline clients before discarding metadata; a checkpoint must not invalidate outstanding edits invisibly. Keep per-document loading lazy and release data according to explicit room/document ownership. Test compaction and reconnect against pinned interoperating versions.\n\nBinary assets use immutable hashes/versions and authenticated, size-bounded transfers with explicit file ownership/leases or conflict resolution. No byte-level CRDT merging of simultaneous sprite/audio edits is promised. Validate paths and symlinks, verify hashes, stage writes atomically and never overwrite an unsaved local file or escape the shared root without an explicit resolution.\n\n\nUse the Hibernation WebSocket API. An idle object may lose all constructor memory while its healthy incoming WebSockets stay connected. Store authoritative permissions/document state durably and retain only small connection identifiers in attachments. Reconstruct authorization on wake; do not trust a stale role copied into an attachment forever.\n\nRuntime restarts/deployments can close sockets, so implement reconnect, replay, deduplication and state restoration independently of hibernation. Avoid event-loop timers and long-lived outbound provider connections in idle room objects. Keep Twitch/YouTube connectors in the client/provider process or a separate service. Coalesce cursor traffic and batch updates within measured latency budgets.\n\nDifferent rooms scale across objects. A single room has a single coordination location and single-threaded execution, with asynchronous operations requiring explicit ordering. Choose initial placement for the team's traffic, measure worldwide latency and shard per-document only when measured load justifies it. Respect the platform's SQL row/blob, storage, message, CPU and connection limits; chunk snapshots and retain large payloads in R2. Version the public Jam protocol independently from Cloudflare's implementation so a later self-hosted backend remains possible; self-hosted delivery is not part of the initial hosted launch.\n\n\nThe room advertises authorized microphone/camera/screen tracks; the backend holds the SFU app secret and performs authorized session/track API mutations. Native/browser endpoints send their media to the SFU and request selected remote tracks. TURN supplies connectivity where required. Clean up the actual SFU tracks/subscriptions when someone leaves, is removed, loses permission or exhausts an applicable budget; deleting discovery metadata alone is insufficient.\n\nAudio first: microphone/output selection, echo cancellation, noise handling, gain, mute/deafen, push-to-talk, per-participant playback level and speaking state. Test wired/Bluetooth/headphone changes and background/foreground behavior. Resume/rejoin failures must not block editing or require restarting the IDE.\n\nVideo next: optional camera tiles, compact/expanded call views, pinning/active speaker, explicit game-window/screen selection and adaptive subscriptions. Decode only the useful quality of visible tiles; release or unsubscribe hidden video while preserving the intended ongoing audio/publication. Hardware encoding/native texture transport is a feasibility question, not an existing RayDE guarantee.\n\nEvaluate native WebRTC and Ashui presentation on macOS, Windows and Linux before committing the implementation stack. RealtimeKit can accelerate a browser proof of concept; its web/mobile UI SDK is not a drop-in native Haxe component. The custom native SFU path needs its own capture, codecs, audio engine, video surfaces and OS permission integration. State the selected encryption/trust model honestly; transport encryption through an SFU is not by itself a promise of end-to-end participant encryption.\n\n\nDefine a proposed registerBroadcastProvider contract and a separate broadcast-driver interface. Providers declare supported features such as account connection, channel status, title/category changes, chat read/send and moderation. The UI only exposes supported capabilities. Names, accounts, credentials and cleanup are namespaced per provider and workspace/session.\n\nOBS is the first driver. Connect to its authenticated WebSocket endpoint, select/preview an existing profile/scene, expose start/stop/reconnect/state and keep broadcast reality authoritative after a disconnect. Initial custom destinations can use an OBS-configured profile; automatic stream-key provisioning and additional encoders are separate future capabilities. Never log keys or silently alter an unrelated OBS configuration.\n\nTwitch is the first named provider: minimal OAuth scopes, EventSub chat/events, supported metadata/status APIs, revocation/reconnect and rate-limit handling. YouTube follows with account/channel selection, broadcast/stream lifecycle and server-streaming live chat, with quota and reconnect handling. Generic/custom destinations retain stream controls without falsely advertising chat/moderation parity. Additional providers such as Kick or self-hosted services are adapters subject to API validation, not mandatory backend dependencies.\n\nFor collaborators on the public broadcast, build a restricted Jam broadcast page/compositor that subscribes only to explicitly approved tracks. OBS can consume it as a browser source. The host selects layout/names/audio mix and can preview before going live. Separate private call audibility from broadcast audibility; prevent duplicate playback/desktop capture and echo. Revoke compositor access and stop forward delivery when consent is withdrawn. Do not expose an editor-capable invitation or reusable account token through an OBS URL.\n\nLocal OBS recording may provide VOD links later. Managed cloud recording/transcription, simultaneous multi-destination restreaming, automatic submission to external jam sites, a public jam marketplace and a full browser code editor are later scope decisions with separate cost/consent work.\n\n\nAll networking, provider calls, native capture, codecs, persistence-heavy work and process management run away from UI handlers. Use event-driven wakeups, cancellation, bounded buffers and explicit resource ownership. Drop stale media frames under backpressure. Never retain a mounted editor, full-size decoded frame or dormant connection solely to show a status item.\n\nBenchmark signed-out startup/idle, many code tabs, active text collaboration, audio-only, camera grid, game-window share, broadcast composition, hidden panels, reconnect and leave/close-all. Record editor and helper process CPU/footprint plus aggregate memory, native/GPU resource counts and frame latency; distinguish retained live resources from allocator high-water. A media/provider process crash must not take down the editing session; a stopped session must release its hooks, workers, devices, subscriptions and native allocations.\n\nRayDE is responsible for its recommended built-in Jam, media and provider components. Third-party native authors remain responsible for their own allocations/cleanup. Native process isolation contains faults; it is not an OS security sandbox for arbitrary approved native code. Source/credentials/SDP/media payloads must not appear in ordinary diagnostic logs. Operational metrics should record identifiers, latency/errors, resource counts and metered usage with documented retention.\n\n\nP0 Foundations: versioned protocol, Cloudflare deployment/secrets/migrations, asynchronous extension services, identity/credential broker, authorization model, Ashui transaction hooks and native media feasibility. Establish performance/abuse test harnesses here.\n\nP1 Private text alpha: durable room lifecycle, native/client CRDT integration, per-author undo, presence/follow, start/join/invite UI, reconnection/recovery and explicit file sharing/checkpoints. Use capped invite-only test rooms; no payment required to validate the architecture.\n\nP2 Voice and streaming pilot: binary assets and shared build artifacts as needed; native audio, SFU authorization/cleanup, provider contracts, OBS and Twitch. Begin shadow usage attribution and cost controls. Preserve local-only operation and fault/idle behavior.\n\nP3 Rich media beta: cameras/game-window sharing, YouTube, custom OBS destinations, consent-aware OBS broadcast composition and browser media guests. Validate actual cross-platform capture/performance and provider policy/authorization behavior.\n\nP4 Hosted launch: proven media accounting, server-enforced grants, payment sandbox/webhook reconciliation, privacy/export/deletion/retention behavior, packaging/user/operator/SDK documentation, migration and recovery drills, load/failure/abuse tests. Launch Community and Jam Plus only after measuring costs and deciding final allowances/prices. Advertise only tested OS/feature combinations.\n\nP5 Team/event expansion: paid team membership and pooled budgets, optional event/theme/deadline/multi-team views, optional preferences sync and evaluation of a prepaid Jam Pass. Enterprise SSO/SLA/managed recording/public discovery remain separately scoped later work.\n\nRelease requires tests for text convergence/Unicode/undo, replay and stale/revoked permissions, crash/hibernation/upgrade recovery, cross-team/window isolation, provider outage, media consent/revocation, output routing/echo, quota races, webhook replay/order, resource cleanup and signed-out idle behavior. Measure server and media costs before confirming pricing. No architecture choice removes the need for client integration and lifecycle tests.\n\n\nEarlier estimates are rough cumulative engineer-time budgets assuming reuse of OBS and existing collaboration/media libraries: streaming-only pilot 3-5 engineer-weeks; small-team Jam alpha approximately 12-20; hardened multi-OS collaboration with integrated voice/private game-window share approximately 24-40. They are not deadlines or commitments and must be revised after the native media, CRDT/editor and billing-metering spikes. Provider review lead times and operating support are additional considerations. Cloudflare reduces backend operations work but does not complete native editor/media integration.\n\nHosting assumptions: the paid Workers minimum is a starting point, with separate compute/storage/account/observability/media usage. Raw SFU/TURN egress was reviewed at $0.05/GB with a shared 1,000 GB provider allowance; budget marginal customer usage beyond that allowance. Four camera publishers at 1 Mbps each, every participant receiving the other three, imply about 5.4 GB per room-hour before audio/overhead and optional compositor subscriptions. Quota accounting is described in the account plan below; client-reported bytes are not a billing authority.\n\n\nThe following account plan is reproduced here so this master node contains the complete current product plan. The existing account workstream and its five issues are associated rather than duplicated.\nRequested scope: plan RayDE account management and product tiers for hosted Jam collaboration, private collaborator audio/video and optional user-selected public streaming. This is a proposed product/implementation plan, not implemented functionality or published pricing. Existing local editor features remain account-free in the proposal.\n\nProduct boundaries\n- Local editing, Rayzor/Haxe language support, Git, build/run, extensions, previews and local history remain available without a RayDE account, including offline. Local OBS control and recording are not subscription gates.\n- An account enables hosted Jam rooms, cloud assets/checkpoints, team membership, optional preferences sync and usage/billing. Signing in does not upload projects, LSP caches or credentials. Content sharing and preferences sync are explicit choices.\n- A Jam has one explicit billing owner: a personal space or a team. Invited guests do not need subscriptions; they count toward the room's participant limit and their usage is charged to that owner. No automatic billing-owner switch when the host disconnects.\n- Public Twitch/YouTube/etc accounts are optional linked identities, separate from RayDE sign-in and team permissions. Membership of a private call does not grant permission to rebroadcast someone.\n\nIdentity and desktop experience\n- Stable internal user IDs, separate provider issuer/subject identities, private verified emails, display names, handles and avatars. Never automatically merge accounts merely because emails match.\n- Launch sign-in with GitHub and email through a managed identity provider behind an OIDC adapter. Select the identity vendor after testing browser redirects, recovery, account export/deletion and pricing. Add passkeys/recovery/MFA where supported, with strong authentication for sensitive ownership/billing actions.\n- Native sign-in uses the system browser with authorization code + PKCE and state; device-code fallback only for providers that support it. Never embed an OAuth client secret in RayDE.\n- App-level AccountManager/CredentialStore coalesces refresh and shares session state across project windows. Each window independently identifies its workspace/team context. OS credential storage owns refresh/provider credentials; ordinary settings/session files contain only non-secret identifiers/preferences. No plaintext token fallback.\n- Accounts settings provides Profile, Connected Accounts, Devices and Sessions, Teams, Plan and Usage, Billing, Export/Delete. Sign-out/revocation releases cloud connections and media while local editing continues. Account/cloud tasks stay off the UI thread and do not add idle polling to a signed-out window.\n- Extensions receive approved profile fields and brokered provider operations, never RayDE bearer tokens. Approved native libraries remain trusted OS code; existing host process isolation is not an OS security sandbox.\n\nAuthorization model\n- Separate user identity, organization membership, billing ownership, Jam role and effective feature/limit grants.\n- Team roles: owner, admin, billing manager, member and guest. Room roles: host/moderator, editor and spectator. Editing, publishing microphone/camera/screen, rebroadcasting and running code/terminal commands are distinct grants.\n- Every room request and media create/subscribe/close operation checks current server authority. Invite tokens are expiring, scoped, revocable and limited-use. Revocation closes existing media subscriptions/tracks as needed, not just hiding them in discovery. Test isolation across personal spaces, teams and parallel project windows.\n\nProvisional tier matrix (USD/month; assumptions to validate with beta measurements)\n- Local: $0, no account, full local IDE; no hosted allocation.\n- Community: $0 account, 4 participants/room including host, 1 simultaneous hosted Jam, 10 GB/month hosted media egress, 1 GB cloud storage, 7-day cloud checkpoint retention.\n- Jam Plus: proposed $12/month, 8 participants/room, 2 simultaneous hosted Jams, 60 GB/month media, 10 GB cloud storage, 30-day cloud checkpoint retention.\n- Teams: proposed $10/paid member/month, minimum 3 paid members; 16 participants/room; simultaneous Jams up to paid member count; 60 GB media and 10 GB storage per paid member pooled across the team; 90-day cloud checkpoint retention; team permissions, consolidated billing and administrative history. Paid members can create/manage team workspaces and rooms; room-scoped guests can collaborate free. Changes to paid seats require explicit billing authority and a charge preview.\n- All hosted tiers include private code collaboration, voice/camera/screen sharing within allowances, and choice of streaming provider. No watermark or resolution paywall; adapt media quality to devices and bandwidth. Public platform distribution is outside RayDE's media meter; guest media delivered into OBS counts as a subscription to those tracks.\n- Launch Community and Jam Plus first. Prepare Teams in the schema but release after invitations, billing ownership and usage enforcement are proven. Consider a prepaid time-limited Jam Pass for occasional game-jam weekends later; price it from measured usage. Do not promise unlimited media, cloud recording, free lifetime cloud hosting or unmeasured SLAs.\n\nPricing and usage rules\n- Media is a pooled egress allowance, with current and estimated consumption visible. Show an indicative remaining-call estimate with bitrate/participant assumptions; never advertise an invariant number of hours for a GB allowance.\n- Cloudflare's current raw SFU/TURN marginal price is $0.05/GB; the 1,000 GB shared provider allowance belongs to our Cloudflare account, not each customer. Model unit costs above that allowance: 10 GB = $0.50 and 60 GB = $3 in media egress alone, before compute, assets, auth, payment fees, support and abuse. Final prices need a unit-economics review.\n- Prototype trusted per-room egress attribution, reporting delay, quota reservations and reconciliation before promising exact per-GB customer billing. Endpoint statistics are estimates, not authoritative invoices. Do not launch postpaid usage charging until attribution is validated. The provider's WebSocket-adapter bytesProcessed is expressly not an authoritative billed total.\n- Reserve bounded usage budgets per active room against one serialized billing-owner ledger, renew/reconcile idempotently, and aggregate accounting away from editor messages. Do not write billing records per keystroke or packet.\n- Default extra spending is disabled. Notify at 80/95 percent, offer quality reduction/audio-only while allowance remains, or explicit prepaid top-ups. At exhaustion block new hosted media and stop active media after a visible, bounded grace/countdown; preserve code collaboration within its own limits and all local work. Never silently charge a guest.\n- Free allocations attach to verified eligible owners, not every disposable workspace; apply account-creation/hosting rate limits, room/connection limits and operator-wide cost controls.\n\nCloudflare and billing architecture\n- Worker API: native/browser auth handoff, sessions, invites, provider OAuth callbacks, entitlement checks and scoped URLs.\n- D1: users, identity mappings, personal/team spaces, memberships, devices, subscription projections and searchable account metadata.\n- JamRoom Durable Object + SQLite: current room authority, connections, document update/checkpoint references, temporary presence and media resource ownership.\n- UsageAccount Durable Object + SQLite: authoritative quota reservations and idempotent usage ledger per personal/team billing owner; D1 receives query/reporting projections.\n- R2: authorized assets, checkpoints and explicit exports; SFU/TURN: collaborator media; Queues: verified billing-event processing, reconciliation and deferred cleanup. Keep streaming adapters behind a provider interface.\n- Stripe hosted Checkout + Customer Portal is the initial proposed payment integration. Verify webhook signatures using raw bodies, deduplicate event IDs, tolerate out-of-order events, retrieve current provider state when reconciling, and keep RayDE-owned versioned entitlements. The checkout return URL and desktop plan labels never grant access. Hosted services enforce feature flags and numerical limits independently of client UI.\n- Upgrades become effective after confirmed billing state; cancellation normally applies at period end; failed payments receive a bounded visible grace policy. Downgrades preserve local files and offer a defined cloud export/over-quota grace period. Define ownership transfer, cloud retention and deletion behavior before public launch.\n\nRollout and acceptance\n1. Identity, secure credential storage and app-level Account settings; preserve signed-out/offline startup and baseline idle behavior.\n2. Personal/team ownership, scoped invitations and server-enforced Jam/media roles in a capped pilot.\n3. Shadow usage accounting and operational budget controls, validated against provider reporting; no variable customer charges.\n4. Versioned entitlements, payment sandbox, signed webhook processing, portal and Community/Plus launch with validated allowances/prices.\n5. Teams, pooled allowances, administrative controls, optional preferences sync and a possible prepaid Jam Pass.\nAcceptance includes account-link takeover/recovery, wrong-team access, expired invites, token refresh concurrency across windows, revoked device/media cleanup, duplicate/out-of-order billing events, quota races across rooms, reconnect/crash recovery, downgrade/export behavior and no authentication work in a signed-out idle editor. Free/paid source sharing remains explicit.\n\nSources reviewed 2026-10-23\nhttps://www.rfc-editor.org/info/rfc8252/\nhttps://docs.github.com/en/apps/oauth-apps/building-oauth-apps/authorizing-oauth-apps\nhttps://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/platform/storage-options/\nhttps://developers.cloudflare.com/realtime/sfu/platform/pricing/\nhttps://developers.cloudflare.com/realtime/sfu/api/\nhttps://developers.cloudflare.com/realtime/sfu/concepts/sessions-tracks/\nhttps://docs.stripe.com/payments/checkout\nhttps://docs.stripe.com/customer-management\nhttps://docs.stripe.com/webhooks\n\n\nDesign sources reviewed during the discussion, baseline 2026-10-23; implementation issues must recheck APIs, limits and prices at implementation time.\nhttps://github.com/y-crdt/y-crdt\nhttps://docs.yjs.dev/ecosystem/connection-provider/y-websocket\nhttps://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/concepts/what-are-durable-objects/\nhttps://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/best-practices/websockets/\nhttps://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/concepts/durable-object-lifecycle/\nhttps://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/platform/limits/\nhttps://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/platform/pricing/\nhttps://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/reference/data-location/\nhttps://developers.cloudflare.com/realtime/sfu/\nhttps://developers.cloudflare.com/realtime/sfu/concepts/sessions-tracks/\nhttps://developers.cloudflare.com/realtime/sfu/platform/pricing/\nhttps://developers.cloudflare.com/realtime/realtimekit/quickstart/\nhttps://webrtc.org/getting-started/turn-server\nhttps://obsproject.com/kb/remote-control-guide\nhttps://github.com/obsproject/obs-websocket/blob/master/docs/generated/protocol.md\nhttps://obsproject.com/kb/browser-source\nhttps://dev.twitch.tv/docs/video-broadcast/\nhttps://dev.twitch.tv/docs/chat\nhttps://developers.google.com/youtube/data/live/getting-started\nhttps://developers.google.com/youtube/data/live/reference/rest/v3/liveChatMessages/streamList\n\n\nThe roadmap index comment lists full git-bug IDs grouped by milestone and explicit blocking dependencies. Every implementation node links back to this master; existing account tasks retain their account-workstream parent as well. All nodes share project:jam. Use git-bug bug --label project:jam to list the roadmap, and phase labels to filter a milestone. Full IDs distinguish git-bug objects from commit SHAs.","files":null}]}